   "morphology": [],
   "translation": {
    "literal": "The one-faced, dark as the blue lotus, old in form, Ramā, dark-bodied in the evening, dwelling in heaven, whose nature is knowledge, of sattva; bearing in her hands the conch, the discus, then the fearlessness-gesture and the tulasī rosary, seated on Tārkṣya — Sarasvatī, the preserver, in the form of Śrī Viṣṇu, I worship.",
    "readable": "At dusk I worship the goddess as Sarasvatī: an old woman with one face, dark as the blue lotus, dark-bodied in the evening light; she is Ramā, dwells in the sky, is knowledge itself, of the quality of sattva; she holds the conch, the discus and a tulasī rosary and shows the gesture of fearlessness; she rides Garuḍa, sustains the world, and has the form of Viṣṇu."
   }
  }
 ],
 "meaning": "The evening form of the goddess of the Gāyatrī in the Mylapore prayoga. After the girl of the morning (Brahmā’s form, creation) and the young woman of midday (Rudra’s form, dissolution), the goddess at dusk is an old woman with Viṣṇu’s attributes — the conch, the discus, a tulasī rosary, Garuḍa as her mount — and she is the one who keeps the world in being. She is named Sarasvatī here, and Ramā; her quality is sattva and her nature knowledge.",
 "in_the_rite": "Step dhyāna of the Gāyatrī japa, evening service only (the Mylapore prayoga), after the general verse muktā-vidruma.",
